// Original practice items. Not drawn from LSAC, PrepTests, or licensed explanations.
const QUESTIONS=[
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Correlation vs. causation","s":"In the seaside town of Brine, umbrella sales rose during the same months the new harbor kiosks were open. The town board concludes that opening the kiosks caused the rise in umbrella sales.","q":"The reasoning in the argument is flawed because the argument","c":["infers that the kiosks caused the sales rise merely because the two occurred in the same months","takes for granted that umbrella sales cannot rise unless a town opens harbor kiosks","treats a requirement for opening a kiosk as though it guaranteed higher umbrella sales","draws a conclusion about every town from interviews with shoppers who already own umbrellas in this instance","assumes that a property of total umbrella sales must be a property of each individual sale"],"a":0,"e":"The only link offered is that kiosk months and higher umbrella sales happened together, and that overlap is treated as proof of causation. The attractive choice, that sales cannot rise unless kiosks open, overreads a one-town causal claim as a necessary condition on every town. The argument never says kiosks are required for a sales rise.","d":1},
{"t":"Assumption","pattern":"Correlation vs. causation","d":1,"s":"Office courtyards that turned on decorative fountains had more songbird visits the next spring than courtyards that left their fountains off. The facilities director concludes that running the fountains caused the additional visits.","q":"The argument depends on assuming which one of the following?","c":["Whenever the cited factor is present, the result in the claim that running the fountains caused the additional visits follows no matter what else differs, here and in every similar case.","Nothing other than running the fountains accounts for the greater number of songbird visits.","Courtyards that already had many birds were the ones most likely to turn fountains on.","Most office courtyards in the region have no fountain.","Most cases outside this one also fit the claim that running the fountains caused the additional visits, whether or not they share its circumstances."],"a":1,"e":"The credited choice is the one that does the job this question asks. The attractive choice — Whenever the cited factor is present, the result in the claim that running the fountains caused the additional visits follows no matter what else differs, here and in every similar case. — loses because it would carry the conclusion if it were granted, but the argument does not need a claim that strong. Sufficient is not the same as necessary."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"Correlation vs. causation","d":1,"s":"Members of a community studio who began using the new slow pottery wheels reported less wrist discomfort after one month than members who kept the old wheels. The studio head concludes that the slow wheels reduce wrist discomfort.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["The side that came out ahead was already ahead before any change, and that earlier gap is the one the records actually show for the claim that the slow wheels reduce wrist discomfort.","Potters who already had comfortable wrists were more likely to switch to the slow wheels.","The two groups had similar wrist discomfort and similar weekly hours before the switch, and neither group changed its clay or its schedule.","Some potters who kept the old wheels also reported less discomfort.","What the argument already reports about the claim that the slow wheels reduce wrist discomfort was recorded by one person, and no rival factor was measured."],"a":2,"e":"A causal conclusion from a difference needs the sides to have been alike apart from that difference. The credited fact is the one that supplies that comparison. The attractive choice — The side that came out ahead was already ahead before any change, and that earlier gap is the one the records actually show for the claim that the slow wheels reduce wrist discomfort. — loses because it is about this comparison, but it makes the conclusion less likely or leaves the gap alone. A fact that cuts against the conclusion is not a strengthener."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"Sufficient/necessary confusion","d":1,"s":"Anyone who completes the dock-safety course receives a green badge. Riley has a green badge. Therefore, Riley completed the dock-safety course.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["That condition is the only way to reach the result, and it was met in the case the claim that Riley completed the dock-safety course describes.","Riley was seen with dock-course materials in the same week the badge was issued.","The result in the claim that Riley completed the dock-safety course was measured with one instrument on every part of the comparison.","Green badges are also issued to inspectors who never take the course, and Riley is an inspector.","The person drawing the claim that Riley completed the dock-safety course expected that result before the comparison was made."],"a":3,"e":"There is another way to get the result, so the condition the argument cites is not required. The attractive choice — That condition is the only way to reach the result, and it was met in the case the claim that Riley completed the dock-safety course describes. — loses because it is the kind of fact that supports the conclusion. It runs in the strengthening direction, so it is not what most weakens the argument."},
{"t":"Parallel","pattern":"Sufficient/necessary confusion","s":"Every potter who receives the kiln certificate has passed the glaze exam. Sam has passed the glaze exam. Therefore, Sam will receive the kiln certificate.","q":"Which one of the following exhibits flawed reasoning most similar to that in the argument above?","c":["Every trail guide who may lead overnight hikes has completed first aid. Nia has not completed first aid. Therefore, Nia may not lead overnight hikes.","Every clerk who may issue a tide permit has passed the chart quiz. Luis has passed the chart quiz. Therefore, Luis may issue a tide permit.","If a clerk may issue a tide permit, then that clerk has passed the chart quiz. Luis may issue a tide permit. Therefore, Luis has passed the chart quiz.","Some people who pass the chart quiz are not clerks. Luis passed the chart quiz, and no other support is offered for that result. Therefore, Luis is not a clerk.","No one who failed the chart quiz may issue a tide permit. Luis failed the chart quiz. Therefore, Luis may not issue a tide permit."],"a":1,"e":"Passing the exam is necessary for the certificate, and the argument treats it as sufficient. The clerk choice makes that same move. The attractive choice, that some quiz-passers are not clerks and therefore Luis is not a clerk, shares the quiz and a negative ending, but it is a \"some, therefore this one is not\" error, not a necessary condition treated as sufficient. The first-aid and failed-quiz choices are valid, and affirming the permit then inferring the quiz is valid too.","d":3},
{"t":"Inference","pattern":"Sufficient/necessary confusion","d":1,"s":"A researcher may handle the unbound maps only if that researcher wears cotton gloves. Dr. Adel wears cotton gloves today. The unbound maps include the 1840 harbor chart.","q":"If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?","c":["The 1840 harbor chart may be handled without cotton gloves, as the argument states it.","Anyone wearing cotton gloves may handle the unbound maps.","Dr. Adel will handle the 1840 harbor chart today.","Dr. Adel may handle the unbound maps today.","If Dr. Adel handles the 1840 harbor chart, then Dr. Adel wears cotton gloves."],"a":4,"e":"The credited statement follows from the statements as they stand. The attractive choice — The 1840 harbor chart may be handled without cotton gloves, as the argument states it. — loses because it adds a cause, a next-year guarantee, or a reversed link. The statements support something narrower."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Unrepresentative sample","s":"A reviewer polled audience members at a weekday matinee of a new play, and nearly all of them said the play was relaxing. The reviewer concludes that nearly all theatergoers find the play relaxing.","q":"The argument is most vulnerable to criticism on the grounds that it","c":["takes a condition necessary for enjoying the play to be sufficient for enjoying it","infers that the play causes relaxation merely because relaxation was reported at one performance","dismisses a contrary review because of that reviewer's personal motives","treats the opinion of a weekday matinee audience as though it stood for theatergoers in general","concludes that the whole audience has a trait established for only one person in it"],"a":3,"e":"A weekday matinee crowd can differ from theatergoers generally, and the conclusion treats that one crowd as the whole group. The attractive choice, that the argument infers the play causes relaxation from a single report, uses the word relaxing but changes the conclusion. The reviewer claims nearly all theatergoers find the play relaxing, not that the play causes relaxation.","d":1},
{"t":"Assumption","pattern":"Unrepresentative sample","d":1,"s":"Volunteers who maintain the east creek path were asked whether the path needs more gravel. Most said yes. The parks office concludes that most people who use the east creek path want more gravel.","q":"Which one of the following is an assumption required by the argument?","c":["People who maintain the path are representative of path users with respect to wanting more gravel.","Whenever the cited factor is present, the result in the claim that most people who use the east creek path want more gravel follows no matter what else differs, here and in every similar case.","A path needs more gravel only if most users request it.","Most people who use the path live near the east creek.","Most cases outside this one also fit the claim that most people who use the east creek path want more gravel, whether or not they share its circumstances."],"a":0,"e":"The credited choice is the one that does the job this question asks. The attractive choice — Whenever the cited factor is present, the result in the claim that most people who use the east creek path want more gravel follows no matter what else differs, here and in every similar case. — loses because it would carry the conclusion if it were granted, but the argument does not need a claim that strong. Sufficient is not the same as necessary."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"Unrepresentative sample","d":1,"s":"On a single midday crossing, staff asked ferry passengers whether the winter schedule should add a dawn trip. Most said yes. The scheduler concludes that most of the ferry's passengers want a dawn trip added.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["The people who were asked differ from the wider group on the very point at issue in the claim that most of the ferry's passengers want a dawn trip added.","Midday passengers are mostly tourists, while a dawn trip would mainly serve commuting workers.","The passengers questioned on that crossing match the ferry's passengers overall in trip purpose, age, and how often they ride.","A few passengers on that crossing said the winter schedule should stay as it is.","Only passengers who already ride at dawn were handed the question card."],"a":2,"e":"The conclusion steps from the people asked to a wider group. A match on the point at issue is what makes that step reasonable. The attractive choice — The people who were asked differ from the wider group on the very point at issue in the claim that most of the ferry's passengers want a dawn trip added. — loses because it is about this comparison, but it makes the conclusion less likely or leaves the gap alone. A fact that cuts against the conclusion is not a strengthener."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"Ad hominem","d":1,"s":"Engineer Okonkwo argues that the cedar footbridge should be closed for repairs. But Okonkwo's sibling runs a boat taxi that gains riders whenever the bridge is closed. So the warning can be dismissed, and the bridge need not be closed.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["A reader with no stake in the outcome went through the same work and reached the claim that the warning can be dismissed, and the bridge need not be closed.","Okonkwo has twice recommended closing a bridge that a later inspection found to be sound.","Some residents prefer the view from the bridge to the view from a boat.","Boat taxis in the area have lost riders over the past year.","An inspection firm with no ferry or taxi business found cracked supports and recommended closing the bridge."],"a":4,"e":"An independent look at the same work reaches the same result, so the motive does not dispose of it. The attractive choice — A reader with no stake in the outcome went through the same work and reached the claim that the warning can be dismissed, and the bridge need not be closed. — loses because it is the kind of fact that supports the conclusion. It runs in the strengthening direction, so it is not what most weakens the argument."},
{"t":"Parallel","pattern":"Ad hominem","s":"Coach Alvarez says the team should replace the sugary drink with water. But Alvarez owns a shop that sells water bottles, so Alvarez's advice should be rejected.","q":"Which one of the following is most similar in its flawed reasoning to the argument above?","c":["Dr. Shah says the clinic should replace its entry rugs. Shah has published studies of slips in clinic entries, so the rugs should be replaced.","Mechanic Rossi says the bus should get new brakes. But Rossi owns a shop that sells brake pads, so Rossi's advice should be rejected.","Nurse Patel says the ward should keep a window closed. Patel's evidence is a mold test, so the window should stay closed.","Every coach who recommends water owns practice gear. Alvarez recommends water, and no other support is offered for that result. Therefore, Alvarez owns practice gear.","Either the team drinks water or it drinks the sugary drink. It should not drink the sugary drink. Therefore, it should drink water."],"a":1,"e":"The stimulus rejects advice only because the adviser might profit. The mechanic choice does the same with brake pads. The attractive choice, that Dr. Shah's rug advice should be followed because Shah published slip studies, has the same \"expert says, therefore do it\" shape at a glance, but the studies are evidence about the rugs rather than a financial stake, so the flaw is not the same.","d":3},
{"t":"Inference","pattern":"Ad hominem","d":1,"s":"Principle: A criticism that cites only a plan's author's motive does not, by itself, show that the plan is unwise. Maren's only criticism of the reservoir plan is that its author wants to be reelected.","q":"If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?","c":["Maren's criticism does not, by itself, show that the reservoir plan is unwise.","The author of the reservoir plan has offered no evidence for it.","A plan whose author wants to be reelected is unwise.","Maren wants the reservoir plan to fail.","The result holds because of those facts, and it would disappear if any one of them were removed, beyond what the claim that a criticism that cites only a plan's author's motive does not, by itself requires."],"a":0,"e":"The credited statement follows from the statements as they stand. The attractive choice — The author of the reservoir plan has offered no evidence for it. — loses because it adds a cause, a next-year guarantee, or a reversed link. The statements support something narrower."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Equivocation","d":3,"s":"A review said the mural was original, meaning the design had not been used before. The town's heritage rule protects an original work, meaning the first physical copy of a design, from demolition. A council member concludes that the mural is protected from demolition because the review called it original.","q":"The argument's reasoning is flawed because it","c":["treats the reviewer as an authority on which works the heritage rule protects, as the argument states it","takes a feature of each painted panel to be a feature of the mural as a whole","relies on one meaning of 'original' in the review and a different meaning of 'original' in the heritage rule","treats the heritage rule as the only possible reason a mural could be kept","rejects the review because the critics might profit if the mural is saved"],"a":2,"e":"The credited description is the mistake this argument makes. The attractive choice — treats the reviewer as an authority on which works the heritage rule protects, as the argument states it — loses because it names a mistake arguments often make, using this argument's subject, but it is not the step this argument takes."},
{"t":"Parallel","pattern":"Equivocation","s":"The coach asked for a sharp practice blade, and by 'sharp' the coach meant a blade that is quick to maneuver. The equipment sheet uses 'sharp' for a blade that cuts easily. This blade is sharp on the equipment sheet. Therefore, it is what the coach asked for.","q":"Which one of the following exhibits flawed reasoning most similar to that in the argument above?","c":["The editor asked for a short note. This piece is a short note, and no other support is offered for that result. Therefore, it is what the editor asked for on those facts alone.","The editor asked for a brief note, and by 'brief' the editor meant a short note. The court clerk uses 'brief' for a written legal argument. This document is a brief in the clerk's sense. Therefore, it is what the editor asked for.","The editor dislikes long notes, so the editor's request for a short note should be ignored. No other support is offered, so the result is taken to follow in every later case of that kind on those facts alone.","Each page of the note is brief, and no other support is offered for that result. Therefore, a bound stack of every page is brief enough to fit in a pocket on those facts alone.","The note was called brief last year, and no other support is offered for that result. Therefore, it will be brief next year on those facts alone."],"a":1,"e":"The stimulus slides from \"sharp\" as quick to maneuver to \"sharp\" as able to cut. The editor choice slides from \"brief\" as a short note to \"brief\" as a legal filing. The attractive choice, that a short note is what the editor asked for because the editor asked for a short note, copies the topic and reaches a matching conclusion, but there is no shift between two meanings, so the reasoning is not the same flaw.","d":3},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"Equivocation","d":3,"s":"The gardener called the soil rich. The nursery uses 'rich' for soil that needs no added fertilizer. A clerk concludes that this soil needs no added fertilizer because the gardener called it rich.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["The two uses of the key word do not share a sense in the argument for the claim that this soil needs no added fertilizer because the gardener called it rich.","The gardener made the remark in a different month from the clerk's conclusion.","Rich soil, in ordinary gardening talk, can still need fertilizer.","By 'rich,' the gardener meant soil that needs no added fertilizer.","By 'rich,' the gardener meant only that the soil looks dark."],"a":3,"e":"The step goes through only when both uses of the word mean the same thing. The attractive choice — The two uses of the key word do not share a sense in the argument for the claim that this soil needs no added fertilizer because the gardener called it rich. — loses because it is about this comparison, but it makes the conclusion less likely or leaves the gap alone. A fact that cuts against the conclusion is not a strengthener."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"False dichotomy","s":"The orchard manager argues: either the trees are covered with netting, or the birds will ruin the entire crop. The trees will not be covered with netting. Therefore, the birds will ruin the entire crop.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["Netting is the most expensive crop protection the orchard has priced in this instance.","Birds are more numerous in the orchard this season than last season.","The manager has long disliked the look of netting.","Orchards that skipped netting and used reflective tape kept their crops.","Some fruit buyers prefer orchards that use netting."],"a":3,"e":"The conclusion depends on there being only two possibilities. Reflective tape that saved crops is a third way, so skipping netting need not mean ruin. The attractive choice, that birds are more numerous this season, feels ominous for the crop. Extra birds lean toward ruin, which supports the manager rather than weakening the manager.","d":1},
{"t":"Parallel","pattern":"False dichotomy","s":"Either the library stays open on Sundays, or students will have no place to study. The library will not stay open on Sundays. Therefore, students will have no place to study.","q":"Which one of the following is most similar in its flawed reasoning to the argument above?","c":["If the clinic hires a night clerk, patients can get bandages after dark. The clinic will hire a night clerk, and no other support is offered for that result. Therefore, patients can get bandages after dark.","Either the clinic stocks the night cabinet, or patients will have no access to bandages after dark. The clinic will not stock the night cabinet. Therefore, patients will have no access to bandages after dark.","The clinic will not stock the night cabinet. A pharmacy next door is open all night, and no other support is offered for that result. Therefore, patients will have some access to bandages after dark.","Most patients want the night cabinet stocked, and no other support is offered for that result. Therefore, the clinic should stock the night cabinet on those facts alone.","Either the clinic stocks the night cabinet, or it keeps its current hours. It will stock the night cabinet, and no other support is offered for that result. Therefore, it will not keep its current hours."],"a":1,"e":"The stimulus treats two options as the only ones and infers the second from rejecting the first. The night-cabinet choice has that shape. The attractive choice, that stocking the cabinet means current hours will be dropped, also looks like an either-or about the clinic. It affirms one option and then denies the other, which is a different mistake from inferring the grim option after rejecting the protective one.","d":3},
{"t":"Inference","pattern":"False dichotomy","d":1,"s":"A tent reserved on the green can host the festival. Hosting it in that tent would not cancel the festival and would not move it into the civic hall. The director claims that the only ways to hold the festival are to cancel it or to move it into the civic hall.","q":"If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?","c":["The director made no claim about the civic hall.","The civic hall cannot hold the festival.","The festival will be held in the tent.","The result holds because of those facts, and it would disappear if any one of them were removed, beyond what the claim that a tent reserved on the green can host the festival requires.","The director's claim is false."],"a":4,"e":"The credited statement follows from the statements as they stand. The attractive choice — The director made no claim about the civic hall. — loses because it adds a cause, a next-year guarantee, or a reversed link. The statements support something narrower."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Part-to-whole","s":"Each of the four cases in the traveling quartet is light enough for one person to carry onto the streetcar. Therefore, the quartet's full set of cases is light enough for one person to carry onto the streetcar.","q":"The argument is most vulnerable to criticism on the grounds that it","c":["appeals to the opinion of streetcar riders who are not experts on luggage","treats a lack of reports about heavy cases as proof that no case is heavy","infers that the set of cases has a feature merely because each case in the set has that feature","takes a condition needed for boarding the streetcar to be enough to guarantee boarding in this instance","concludes that what was true of the cases on past trips will be true on the next trip"],"a":2,"e":"Four light cases can make a heavy set. The argument gives the whole a feature shown only for the parts. The attractive choice, that a condition needed for boarding is treated as enough to guarantee boarding, borrows \"streetcar\" and a familiar conditional flaw. No boarding requirement is stated or misused; the leap is from each case to the set.","d":1},
{"t":"Assumption","pattern":"Part-to-whole","d":1,"s":"Each map in the trail packet is small enough to fold into a pocket. Therefore, the trail packet is small enough to fold into a pocket.","q":"The argument depends on assuming which one of the following?","c":["The trail packet itself is small enough to fold into a pocket if every map in it is.","Maps that fold into a pocket are easier to read than maps that do not in this instance.","The hiker will not carry anything other than the trail packet.","Most hikers own pockets large enough for several maps.","The trail packet contains only one map."],"a":0,"e":"The credited choice is the one that does the job this question asks. The attractive choice — Maps that fold into a pocket are easier to read than maps that do not in this instance. — loses because it would carry the conclusion if it were granted, but the argument does not need a claim that strong. Sufficient is not the same as necessary."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"Part-to-whole","d":1,"s":"Each pump in the plaza fountain operates below the town's quiet limit. The engineer concludes that the fountain as a whole operates below that limit.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["Taken together, the parts go past the limit each part meets on its own, in the situation described by the claim that the fountain as a whole operates below that limit.","The fountain is louder than its loudest pump, because the basin adds its own noise.","The quiet limit was set by the town council, not by the engineer.","What the argument already reports about the claim that the fountain as a whole operates below that limit was recorded by one person, and no rival factor was measured.","The fountain's noise equals the noise of its loudest pump, and no other part of the fountain makes sound."],"a":4,"e":"If the whole does not exceed its most extreme part, that part meeting the limit settles the whole. The attractive choice — Taken together, the parts go past the limit each part meets on its own, in the situation described by the claim that the fountain as a whole operates below that limit. — loses because it is about this comparison, but it makes the conclusion less likely or leaves the gap alone. A fact that cuts against the conclusion is not a strengthener."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Circular reasoning","s":"The revised tide table is reliable. After all, it matches the harbormaster's readings, and those readings are reliable because they match the revised tide table.","q":"The reasoning in the argument is flawed because the argument","c":["infers a cause from the mere fact that the table and the readings agree","draws a conclusion about all tide tables from this one table","appeals to the harbormaster's fame rather than to an independent check","supports its claim with a further claim that depends on the claim being supported","treats a drop in the percentage of accurate entries as a drop in their number in this instance"],"a":3,"e":"The table is called reliable because of the readings, and the readings are called reliable because of the table. Each side leans on the other. The attractive choice, that a cause is inferred from mere agreement, catches the word \"matches\" and invents a causal claim. The argument is not saying one source caused the other; it is letting them certify each other.","d":3},
{"t":"Parallel","pattern":"Circular reasoning","s":"The bakery scale is accurate because it matches the recipe card's weights, and the recipe card's weights are accurate because they match the bakery scale.","q":"Which one of the following exhibits flawed reasoning most similar to that in the argument above?","c":["The stage clock is right because it matches the conductor's watch, and the watch is right because it matches the stage clock.","The stage clock matches a studio clock calibrated last week, and no other support is offered for that result. Therefore, the stage clock is right.","The stage clock was wrong last year, and no other support is offered for that result. Therefore, it is wrong now.","A famous conductor says the stage clock is right, and no other support is offered for that result. Therefore, the stage clock is right.","Either the stage clock is right, or the cue will be missed. The cue was not missed. Therefore, the stage clock is right."],"a":0,"e":"The stimulus lets the scale and the recipe card vouch for each other. The stage clock and the watch do the same. The attractive choice, that a famous conductor's say-so shows the clock is right, feels like a flaw in the same neighborhood. It is an appeal to fame, not two claims leaning on each other.","d":3},
{"t":"Inference","pattern":"Circular reasoning","d":3,"s":"A set of remarks that offers a claim as its only ground does not establish that claim. Oren's only ground for saying the clock-tower bell is on time is that the clock-tower bell is on time.","q":"If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?","c":["Oren checked the bell against another clock.","The clock-tower bell is not on time.","Oren's remarks do not establish that the clock-tower bell is on time.","The result holds because of those facts, and it would disappear if any one of them were removed, beyond what the claim that a set of remarks that offers a claim as its only ground does not establish that claim requires.","The same link will hold next year in every case like the claim that a set of remarks that offers a claim as its only ground does not establish that claim."],"a":2,"e":"The credited statement follows from the statements as they stand. The attractive choice — Oren checked the bell against another clock. — loses because it adds a cause, a next-year guarantee, or a reversed link. The statements support something narrower."},
{"t":"Inference","pattern":"Appeal to authority","d":1,"s":"A recommendation about bridge cables is not established merely because a celebrated pastry chef makes it. The only support offered for replacing the bridge cables is that a celebrated pastry chef recommended the replacement.","q":"If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?","c":["The result holds because of those facts, and it would disappear if any one of them were removed, beyond what the claim that a recommendation about bridge cables is not established merely because a celebrated pastry chef requires.","A recommendation made by an engineer would also fail to establish that the cables should be replaced.","The bridge cables should not be replaced.","The same link will hold next year in every case like the claim that a recommendation about bridge cables is not established merely because a celebrated pastry chef.","The support offered does not establish that the bridge cables should be replaced."],"a":4,"e":"The credited statement follows from the statements as they stand. The attractive choice — The result holds because of those facts, and it would disappear if any one of them were removed, beyond what the claim that a recommendation about bridge cables is not established merely because a celebrated pastry chef requires. — loses because it adds a cause, a next-year guarantee, or a reversed link. The statements support something narrower."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"Appeal to authority","d":1,"s":"A famous marathon champion says the city's drinking water should be fluoridated. Therefore, the city should fluoridate its drinking water.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["Regional water engineers studied this supply and recommended that it not be fluoridated.","The person cited applied this field's own standard to this case before the claim that the city should fluoridate its drinking water was drawn.","Some residents dislike the taste of fluoridated water.","The champion drinks the city's tap water every day.","A neighboring city fluoridates its drinking water."],"a":0,"e":"A source applying the proper standard points the other way, which undercuts a bare citation. The attractive choice — The person cited applied this field's own standard to this case before the claim that the city should fluoridate its drinking water was drawn. — loses because it is the kind of fact that supports the conclusion. It runs in the strengthening direction, so it is not what most weakens the argument."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"Appeal to authority","d":1,"s":"Dr. Feldman, author of the regional manual on harbor pilings, says the elm pilings should be replaced this year. The harbor board concludes that the elm pilings should be replaced this year.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["The person cited for the claim that the elm pilings should be replaced this year is known for a neighboring field and never applied this field's standard to this case.","Feldman's recommendation applies the measurements and replacement rules in that manual to the elm pilings as they stand this year.","A local newspaper printed Feldman's remark without comment.","Several board members have not read the manual.","What the argument already reports about the claim that the elm pilings should be replaced this year was recorded by one person, and no rival factor was measured."],"a":1,"e":"A citation helps only when this source applied the relevant standard to this case. The attractive choice — The person cited for the claim that the elm pilings should be replaced this year is known for a neighboring field and never applied this field's standard to this case. — loses because it is about this comparison, but it makes the conclusion less likely or leaves the gap alone. A fact that cuts against the conclusion is not a strengthener."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"Ignoring alternatives","d":1,"s":"The garden path was underwater this morning. The groundskeeper concludes that the irrigation line was left open overnight.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["Before the change, the two sides already matched on the result and on the other factors relevant to the claim that the irrigation line was left open overnight, and the cited change was the only difference.","The groundskeeper has worked in the garden for many years.","A storm dropped three inches of rain on the garden overnight, and the irrigation valve stayed closed.","The irrigation line is painted green to match the hedges.","An open irrigation line can leave a path underwater."],"a":2,"e":"The conclusion treats one difference as the reason the results diverged. The credited fact gives a rival reason, or shows the gap does not depend on that difference. The attractive choice — Before the change, the two sides already matched on the result and on the other factors relevant to the claim that the irrigation line was left open overnight, and the cited change was the only difference. — loses because it is the kind of fact that supports the conclusion. It runs in the strengthening direction, so it is not what most weakens the argument."},
{"t":"Assumption","pattern":"Ignoring alternatives","d":1,"s":"The reading-room lamp went out during the lecture. The host concludes that the bulb has burned out.","q":"Which one of the following is an assumption required by the argument?","c":["Whenever the cited factor is present, the result in the claim that the bulb has burned out follows no matter what else differs, here and in every similar case.","Burned-out bulbs are more common in reading rooms than in hallways.","The host has replaced bulbs in the reading room before.","No other cause, such as a tripped breaker or a switched-off socket, accounts for the lamp going out.","The lecture continued after the lamp went out."],"a":3,"e":"The credited choice is the one that does the job this question asks. The attractive choice — Whenever the cited factor is present, the result in the claim that the bulb has burned out follows no matter what else differs, here and in every similar case. — loses because it would carry the conclusion if it were granted, but the argument does not need a claim that strong. Sufficient is not the same as necessary."},
{"t":"Inference","pattern":"Ignoring alternatives","d":1,"s":"The bakery oven stopped heating. A burned-out element, a tripped breaker, or a gas shutoff could each stop the oven. The notes contain no further information about which of those occurred.","q":"If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?","c":["The result holds because of those facts, and it would disappear if any one of them were removed, beyond what the claim that the bakery oven stopped heating requires.","None of the three causes could stop this particular oven.","The oven stopped heating because of a gas shutoff.","The same link will hold next year in every case like the claim that the bakery oven stopped heating.","The notes do not establish whether the element, the breaker, or the gas shutoff stopped the oven."],"a":4,"e":"The credited statement follows from the statements as they stand. The attractive choice — The result holds because of those facts, and it would disappear if any one of them were removed, beyond what the claim that the bakery oven stopped heating requires. — loses because it adds a cause, a next-year guarantee, or a reversed link. The statements support something narrower."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Percentages vs. numbers","d":3,"s":"Last year, bikes were 40 percent of the vehicles in the station garage. This year, bikes are 25 percent of the vehicles there. Therefore, the garage holds fewer bikes this year than last year.","q":"The reasoning in the argument is flawed because the argument","c":["overlooks the possibility that the total number of vehicles in the garage increased enough to offset the smaller share of bikes","treats the garage's past share of bikes as proof that the same share will continue, as the argument states it","takes the absence of a newer count as proof that the number of bikes is unchanged","assumes that a feature of each bike is a feature of the whole garage","rejects the count because of who recorded it"],"a":0,"e":"The argument reads a change in share as a change in number without settling what happened to the totals. The attractive choice — treats the garage's past share of bikes as proof that the same share will continue, as the argument states it — loses because it names a mistake arguments often make, using this argument's subject, but it is not the step this argument takes."},
{"t":"Assumption","pattern":"Percentages vs. numbers","d":1,"s":"Seed packets fell from 50 percent of the items sold at a nursery to 30 percent. The buyer concludes that the nursery sold fewer seed packets.","q":"The argument depends on assuming which one of the following?","c":["The later total was smaller than the earlier total, not merely no larger, for the claim that the nursery sold fewer seed packets.","The total number of items sold did not increase enough to offset seed packets' smaller share of sales.","The nursery sold no items other than seed packets and flowering plants.","Most customers who buy seed packets buy nothing else.","Most cases outside this one also fit the claim that the nursery sold fewer seed packets, whether or not they share its circumstances."],"a":1,"e":"The credited choice is the one that does the job this question asks. The attractive choice — The later total was smaller than the earlier total, not merely no larger, for the claim that the nursery sold fewer seed packets. — loses because it would carry the conclusion if it were granted, but the argument does not need a claim that strong. Sufficient is not the same as necessary."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"Percentages vs. numbers","d":3,"s":"At the reef museum, postcards dropped from 20 percent of gift-shop items sold to 10 percent. The buyer concludes that fewer postcards were sold.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["The later total was several times the earlier total, large enough that a smaller share need not mean a smaller count for the claim that fewer postcards were sold.","The later period included a holiday weekend, and the earlier period did not.","The gift shop sold the same number of items in both periods.","The shop added a new line of stuffed fish during the later period.","Some visitors bought more than one postcard."],"a":2,"e":"A smaller share is a smaller count when the total does not grow enough to offset the drop. The credited fact keeps the total from doing that. The attractive choice — The later total was several times the earlier total, large enough that a smaller share need not mean a smaller count for the claim that fewer postcards were sold. — loses because it is about this comparison, but it makes the conclusion less likely or leaves the gap alone. A fact that cuts against the conclusion is not a strengthener."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"Past-to-future","d":1,"s":"The cedar footbridge has supported the harvest parade float every year for a decade. Therefore, it will support this year's float.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["The upcoming case sits inside the range of the earlier successes, and nothing relevant has changed for the claim that it will support this year's float.","The parade has used the footbridge rather than the main road for the whole decade.","Crowd estimates for the parade have been reliable in past years.","This year's float weighs far more than any float the bridge has carried.","The float is decorated with the same colors every year."],"a":3,"e":"The next case is relevantly different, so the past run does not reach it. The attractive choice — The upcoming case sits inside the range of the earlier successes, and nothing relevant has changed for the claim that it will support this year's float. — loses because it is the kind of fact that supports the conclusion. It runs in the strengthening direction, so it is not what most weakens the argument."},
{"t":"Parallel","pattern":"Past-to-future","s":"This bus route has arrived on time every weekday this season. So it will arrive on time next weekday.","q":"Which one of the following exhibits flawed reasoning most similar to that in the argument above?","c":["The bus was late yesterday, and no other support is offered for that result. Therefore, it was late on the weekdays before yesterday.","This kiln has reached the target temperature on every firing this month. So it will reach the target temperature on the next firing.","The kiln reached the target temperature and the bread rose, and no other support is offered for that result. Therefore, the temperature caused the bread to rise.","If the kiln reaches the target temperature, the firing is finished. This firing is finished. Therefore, the kiln reached the target temperature.","The bus was on time today because the driver is careful, and the driver is careful because the bus was on time today. No other support is offered, so the result is taken to follow in every later case of that kind."],"a":1,"e":"The stimulus projects a recent unbroken record one step ahead. The kiln choice does the same. The attractive choice, that reaching temperature caused the bread to rise, puts the kiln in a causal story. The stimulus is not inferring a cause from two events. It is extending a streak.","d":3},
{"t":"Inference","pattern":"Past-to-future","d":1,"s":"That a ferry schedule ran without delays last summer does not, by itself, show that the same schedule will run without delays this summer. The planning memo's only reason for predicting no delays this summer is that the same schedule had no delays last summer.","q":"If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?","c":["Last summer's weather will be repeated this summer.","The schedule will have delays this summer.","The result holds because of those facts, and it would disappear if any one of them were removed, beyond what the claim that a ferry schedule ran without delays last summer does not, by itself requires.","The same link will hold next year in every case like the claim that a ferry schedule ran without delays last summer does not, by itself.","The planning memo's reason does not, by itself, show that the schedule will run without delays this summer."],"a":4,"e":"The credited statement follows from the statements as they stand. The attractive choice — Last summer's weather will be repeated this summer. — loses because it adds a cause, a next-year guarantee, or a reversed link. The statements support something narrower."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Absence of evidence","s":"No inspection of the town archive has reported silverfish. Therefore, the archive contains no silverfish.","q":"The argument is most vulnerable to criticism on the grounds that it","c":["bases the conclusion on inspections of a building that is unlike the archive","treats the inspectors' possible bias as a reason to reject their reports","infers that the archive has a feature because each room in it has that feature","treats the lack of reports of silverfish as though it established that silverfish are absent","assumes that a word used in the inspection logs has the same sense in ordinary speech in this instance"],"a":3,"e":"Not having a report of silverfish is a gap, and the argument treats that gap as proof of absence. The attractive choice, that the inspectors' possible bias is a reason to reject their reports, brings in a motive the argument never uses. No inspector is dismissed. Silence is promoted into a clean bill.","d":1},
{"t":"Assumption","pattern":"Absence of evidence","d":1,"s":"No tester has found a leak in the blue canoe. The outfitter concludes that the blue canoe has no leak.","q":"Which one of the following is an assumption required by the argument?","c":["If the blue canoe had a leak, a tester would have found it.","Whenever the cited factor is present, the result in the claim that the blue canoe has no leak follows no matter what else differs, here and in every similar case.","The blue canoe is rented more often than the red canoe.","The outfitter has never found a leak in any canoe.","A canoe can have at most one leak."],"a":0,"e":"The credited choice is the one that does the job this question asks. The attractive choice — Whenever the cited factor is present, the result in the claim that the blue canoe has no leak follows no matter what else differs, here and in every similar case. — loses because it would carry the conclusion if it were granted, but the argument does not need a claim that strong. Sufficient is not the same as necessary."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"Absence of evidence","d":1,"s":"No shift log at the planetarium mentions a jammed dome motor. The director concludes that the dome motor has not jammed.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["The search behind the claim that the dome motor has not jammed looked where such an event is rarely written down, so a real instance could sit in a record that was not read.","The dome motor is louder than the projector fan.","Every jam of the dome motor would have been entered in the shift log, and the logs for the period in question are complete.","A jammed motor would be expensive to replace.","The director wrote some of the shift logs."],"a":2,"e":"Silence supports absence only when an instance would have been recorded and the record was read. The attractive choice — The search behind the claim that the dome motor has not jammed looked where such an event is rarely written down, so a real instance could sit in a record that was not read. — loses because it is about this comparison, but it makes the conclusion less likely or leaves the gap alone. A fact that cuts against the conclusion is not a strengthener."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"Conditional reversal/negation","d":3,"s":"If a cabin is rented in January, then it is winterized. Cabin 7 is winterized. Therefore, cabin 7 is rented in January.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["That condition is the only way to reach the result, and it was met in the case the claim that cabin 7 is rented in January describes.","Every cabin rented in January is inspected in December.","Cabins that are not winterized are rented in July.","Cabin 7 is the only winterized cabin on the lake.","Cabin 7 was winterized for its owner's private use and is not offered for rent in January."],"a":4,"e":"There is another way to get the result, so the condition the argument cites is not required. The attractive choice — That condition is the only way to reach the result, and it was met in the case the claim that cabin 7 is rented in January describes. — loses because it is the kind of fact that supports the conclusion. It runs in the strengthening direction, so it is not what most weakens the argument."},
{"t":"Parallel","pattern":"Conditional reversal/negation","s":"If a loaf is on the morning rack, then it was baked before dawn. This loaf was baked before dawn. Therefore, this loaf is on the morning rack.","q":"Which one of the following exhibits flawed reasoning most similar to that in the argument above?","c":["If a painting is in the east gallery, then it is framed in oak. This painting is framed in oak. Therefore, this painting is in the east gallery.","If a painting is in the east gallery, then it is framed in oak. This painting is in the east gallery. Therefore, it is framed in oak.","If a painting is in the east gallery, then it is framed in oak. This painting is not framed in oak. Therefore, it is not in the east gallery.","If a painting is in the east gallery, then it is framed in oak. This painting is not in the east gallery. Therefore, it is not framed in oak.","Some paintings framed in oak are not in the east gallery. This painting is framed in oak, and no other support is offered for that result. Therefore, it is not in the east gallery."],"a":0,"e":"The stimulus affirms the necessary result and infers the sufficient condition. The oak-frame choice that starts from the frame and infers the gallery does the same. The attractive choice, that a painting in the gallery is therefore oak-framed, uses the same rule and the valid direction. Similar wording is not the same flaw.","d":3},
{"t":"Inference","pattern":"Conditional reversal/negation","s":"If a parcel is stored in the river warehouse, then it is logged in the blue ledger. Parcel K is not logged in the blue ledger. Parcel M is logged in the blue ledger.","q":"If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?","c":["Parcel M is stored in the river warehouse.","Parcel K is logged in some ledger other than the blue ledger.","No parcel logged in the blue ledger is stored in the river warehouse.","Parcel K is not stored in the river warehouse.","Parcel M is not stored in the river warehouse."],"a":3,"e":"Storage in the warehouse is sufficient for a blue-ledger entry, so a parcel with no such entry was not stored there. The attractive choice, that parcel M was stored in the warehouse, affirms the ledger line and runs the conditional backward. M's entry is what an stored parcel would have, not proof that M was stored.","d":3},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Analogy","s":"Banning phones in the central library's reading room reduced noise there. Therefore, banning phones in the city hospital's wards will reduce noise in the wards.","q":"The argument is most vulnerable to criticism on the grounds that it","c":["concludes that the ban caused the quieter reading room merely because the ban and the quiet occurred together in this instance","treats the reading room and the wards as interchangeable without showing that they are alike in ways that matter to noise","takes a rule that is necessary for a quiet reading room to be sufficient for a quiet ward","bases the conclusion on one afternoon in the reading room that was unlike other afternoons","infers that the hospital as a whole will be quiet because one ward would be quiet"],"a":1,"e":"A phone ban that helped a reading room need not help a hospital ward. The argument leans on that comparison without a relevant likeness. The attractive choice, that the ban is inferred to have caused the quiet merely because the two occurred together, attacks a causal reading of the premise. The argument offers the reading-room reduction as a premise and then transfers it. The unsupported step is the transfer.","d":1},
{"t":"Parallel","pattern":"Analogy","s":"Adding grit to the studio path stopped puddles on that path. So adding grit to the rooftop running track will stop puddles on the track.","q":"Which one of the following exhibits flawed reasoning most similar to that in the argument above?","c":["The track is steeper than the path, so grit that stopped puddles on the path may fail on the track. No other support is offered, so the result is taken to follow in every later case of that kind.","Grit stopped puddles on the path, and the track sheds water in the same way, and no other support is offered for that result. Therefore, grit will stop puddles on the track.","Each section of the path is dry, and no other support is offered for that result. Therefore, the whole path is dry.","Puddles became rare after grit was added, and the rain also stopped, and no other support is offered for that result. Therefore, the grit caused the change.","Adding shade cloth to the herb beds stopped leaf scorch there. So adding shade cloth to the greenhouse glass will stop leaf scorch there."],"a":4,"e":"The stimulus moves a fix from one surface to a different surface with no relevant likeness. Shade cloth from herb beds to greenhouse glass does the same. The attractive choice, that grit will stop track puddles because the track sheds water the same way, keeps the topic and supplies the likeness the stimulus lacks, so it is not the same flawed move.","d":3},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"Analogy","d":1,"s":"Requiring visitors to sign a clipboard reduced lost badges at the small craft museum. The director concludes that the same clipboard rule will reduce lost badges at the riverside science hall.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["The new setting does not work by the mechanism that produced the earlier result cited for the claim that the same clipboard rule will reduce lost badges at the riverside science hall.","The craft museum started the clipboard rule during a holiday week.","At both sites, badges are lost when visitors pass an unattended desk, and the desks are staffed in the same way.","The science hall has more visitors per day than the craft museum.","Clipboard paper at the craft museum was replaced every evening."],"a":2,"e":"The transfer is reasonable when the two settings work the same way. The credited fact says the mechanism matches. The attractive choice — The new setting does not work by the mechanism that produced the earlier result cited for the claim that the same clipboard rule will reduce lost badges at the riverside science hall. — loses because it is about this comparison, but it makes the conclusion less likely or leaves the gap alone. A fact that cuts against the conclusion is not a strengthener."}
];
if (typeof BANK_PARTS !== 'undefined') { for (const part of BANK_PARTS) for (const q of part) QUESTIONS.push(q); }
